section "Backtick en el info string que es texto"
  text line 51: "```js` es un code span suelto"
  text line 53: "Fin."
CODE002 line 15: the code block opened with 3 backticks closes at "````", a line of 4 backticks; earlier versions kept that line as code. To keep it as code, open the block with more backticks than it has
CODE003 line 23: the code block opened with 4 backticks is closed by a 3-backtick line (line 25), as in earlier versions; CommonMark would not close it there. Close it with 4 backticks
CODE003 line 29: this line has a backtick in its info string, so CommonMark reads it as text; it is read as a code block that closes at line 31, as in earlier versions, because as text the rest would leave a code block unclosed. Remove the backtick from the info string
CODE003 line 35: the code block opened with 4 backticks is closed by a 3-backtick line (line 38), as in earlier versions; CommonMark would not close it there. Close it with 4 backticks
CODE003 line 41: the code block opened with 4 backticks is closed by a 3-backtick line (line 43), as in earlier versions; CommonMark would not close it there. Close it with 4 backticks
CODE004 line 51: this line starts like a code block but has a backtick in its info string, so it is text (CommonMark); earlier versions read it as a code block that was never closed
CODE004 line 7: the code block opened with 4 backticks closes at line 11; earlier versions closed it at line 10
